WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.676
Coding agents are quick to say a bug is fixed.

00:00:03.726 --> 00:00:06.463
VeriFix is an agent that has to prove it.

00:00:06.513 --> 00:00:08.570
I'm Shivansh, from Team HyperX.

00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:11.764
Judges start on the Verify screen.

00:00:11.814 --> 00:00:18.983
It checks seven claims live, against GitHub, neatlogs and our own run records, and links the evidence behind each one.

00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:21.997
Now, a live run on AWS Lambda.

00:00:22.047 --> 00:00:22.587
The bug:

00:00:22.637 --> 00:00:27.457
a ledger fee function that loses cents to floating-point rounding.

00:00:27.507 --> 00:00:34.025
Before editing anything, VeriFix uses Entire Graph to find every caller of that function.

00:00:34.075 --> 00:00:37.124
For this demo, I inject a bad first patch.

00:00:37.174 --> 00:00:41.256
It passes the bug's own test, but it breaks two callers.

00:00:42.204 --> 00:00:47.579
VeriFix runs the caller tests too, catches the regression, and rolls the workspace back.

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:51.523
The second attempt fixes the bug and keeps every caller passing.

00:00:51.573 --> 00:00:54.667
Only then does it issue a verification certificate.

00:00:56.775 --> 00:00:59.158
Every run sends a trace to neatlogs:

00:00:59.208 --> 00:01:02.063
each model call, tool step, token and cost.

00:01:02.113 --> 00:01:04.630
Those traces drove our biggest change.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:08.482
Our first version stuffed the repository into the prompt:

00:01:08.532 --> 00:01:11.117
thirty-one thousand tokens for one fix.

00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:16.860
With caller context from Entire Graph, the same fix takes nine hundred and forty-six.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:21.725
That's ninety-seven percent fewer tokens, and ninety-six percent faster.

00:01:22.875 --> 00:01:24.173
Entire does two jobs.

00:01:24.223 --> 00:01:28.663
At run time, Entire Graph tells the agent what its change could break.

00:01:28.713 --> 00:01:36.618
During development, Entire checkpoints recorded the agent sessions behind our commits, so you can see how VeriFix was built.

00:01:37.575 --> 00:01:44.136
Our business plan, in the cfo.ai planning format, starts from the token usage we measured in neatlogs.

00:01:44.186 --> 00:01:48.089
The twenty-four month projection breaks even in month eleven.

00:01:48.139 --> 00:01:51.265
And the stress test shows why efficiency matters:

00:01:51.315 --> 00:01:54.505
without graph context, model costs exceed revenue.

00:01:55.454 --> 00:01:57.719
We also looked at a real codebase:

00:01:57.769 --> 00:01:58.876
the neatlogs SDK.

00:01:58.926 --> 00:02:08.544
We found telemetry being dropped under concurrent load, reported it as issue one ninety-nine, and opened pull request two oh three with a fix.

00:02:09.893 --> 00:02:10.396
VeriFix:

00:02:10.446 --> 00:02:13.369
a coding agent that has to prove its fixes.

00:02:13.419 --> 00:02:15.650
Every number you've seen is live.

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:18.829
Open the Verify screen, and check it yourself.

